---
import Layout from "@components/Layout.astro";
import PageHeader from "@components/PageHeader.astro";
import CardAnalysis from "@components/CardAnalysis.astro";
import { Card } from "webcoreui/astro";
import DecklistTooltip from "@components/DecklistTooltip.astro";
---

<Layout title="Sideboard Analysis - Replenish Guide">
    <PageHeader
        title="Sideboard Analysis"
        description="Mastering competitive Replenish demands an adaptive, disciplined 15-card toolkit capable of transitioning between explosive combo setups and hard-hitting creature transformations."
        imageSrc="/src/assets/banners/attunement.jpeg"
        imageAlt="Attunement"
        imageCaption="Attunement by Randy Gallegos"
        isCard={true}
    />

    <Card title="The Sculpting Methodology" titleTag="h2">
        <p>
            A disciplined tournament pilot relies on a systematic methodology
            before every regional or competitive championship to finalize their
            75. Rather than constructing a sideboard from scratch on tournament
            morning, begin with a master pool of 85–90 proven cards, then
            analytically carve away options that do not align with expected
            local metagame concentrations.
        </p>
        <ol style="padding-left: 1.25rem;">
            <li style="margin-bottom: 0.35rem;">
                <strong>Start Broad:</strong> Maintain an expansive candidate pool
                of 85–90 cards, encompassing archetype variants and specific bullet
                interaction.
            </li>
            <li style="margin-bottom: 0.35rem;">
                <strong>Assess the Metagame:</strong> Heavy red and aggressive tribal
                fields demand sweeping board control (<a
                    href="#"
                    class="card-hover"
                    data-card-name="Wrath of God">Wrath of God</a
                >, <a href="#" class="card-hover" data-card-name="Sphere of Law"
                    >Sphere of Law</a
                >). Permission-saturated blue tables necessitate mana taxing (<a
                    href="#"
                    class="card-hover"
                    data-card-name="Defense Grid">Defense Grid</a
                >) and robust midrange threat structures.
            </li>
            <li style="margin-bottom: 0.35rem;">
                <strong>Prioritize Utility Slots:</strong> Rank flex options by standalone
                power level and multi-matchup application.
            </li>
            <li style="margin-bottom: 0.35rem;">
                <strong>Cut Strategically:</strong> Eliminate targeted bullets that
                fail to actively serve across at least two major matchup profiles.
            </li>
            <li style="margin-bottom: 0.35rem;">
                <strong>Finalize Transformative Packages:</strong> Deploy post-board
                pivot strategies only when they invert undesirable Game 1 dynamics
                across multiple foundational archetypes.
            </li>
            <li>
                <strong>Main vs. Side Positioning:</strong> Assign final positions
                based on mathematical expected matchup frequencies.
            </li>
        </ol>
        <p style="margin-top: 1rem; margin-bottom: 0;">
            <strong>The Creature Sideboard Package:</strong> Incorporating <a
                href="#"
                class="card-hover"
                data-card-name="Exalted Angel">Exalted Angel</a
            > and <a href="#" class="card-hover" data-card-name="Meddling Mage"
                >Meddling Mage</a
            > enables an aggressive, high-efficiency midrange transformation after
            sideboarding. This places opponents in an untenable structural bind: if
            they retain heavy anti-enchantment and graveyard hate, they succumb quickly
            to aerial beatdowns; if they dilute their hate to respect creatures, they
            are overrun by the namesake combo.
        </p>
    </Card>

    <Card title="Sideboarding Principles" titleTag="h2">
        <h3 style="margin-top: 0;">Transformation Against Control</h3>
        <p>
            Against heavy blue permission archetypes, the deck seamlessly
            transitions its primary identity. Velocity engines like <a
                href="#"
                class="card-hover"
                data-card-name="Frantic Search">Frantic Search</a
            >, reactive lock pieces like <a
                href="#"
                class="card-hover"
                data-card-name="Parallax Wave">Parallax Wave</a
            >, and excess spot removal exit the 75. In their place, <a
                href="#"
                class="card-hover"
                data-card-name="Defense Grid">Defense Grid</a
            >, <a href="#" class="card-hover" data-card-name="Exalted Angel"
                >Exalted Angel</a
            >, <a href="#" class="card-hover" data-card-name="Abeyance"
                >Abeyance</a
            >, <a href="#" class="card-hover" data-card-name="Arcane Denial"
                >Arcane Denial</a
            >, and full rosters of <a
                href="#"
                class="card-hover"
                data-card-name="Meddling Mage">Meddling Mage</a
            > take over. Operating as a proactive midrange beatdown deck completely
            invalidates traditional control post-board logic.
        </p>

        <h3>Maintaining the Combo Against Aggression</h3>
        <p>
            When pairing against linear aggressive builds, the primary combo
            engine remains completely intact to facilitate an explosive race.
            Swap situational interaction slots; specifically, trim <a
                href="#"
                class="card-hover"
                data-card-name="Seal of Cleansing">Seal of Cleansing</a
            > whenever opponents lack relevant enchantment or artifact targets.
        </p>

        <h3>Retiring Mages Against Green and Midrange</h3>
        <p>
            Against heavy green midrange formations and attrition suites (e.g.,
            The Rock, RG Ponza, Terrageddon), <a
                href="#"
                class="card-hover"
                data-card-name="Meddling Mage">Meddling Mage</a
            > exits the formation. Opposing creature curves consistently outclass
            a 2/2 body, and their strategy rarely bottlenecks upon a single named
            spell. Transition into <a
                href="#"
                class="card-hover"
                data-card-name="Exalted Angel">Exalted Angel</a
            > to dominate combat math and life-point races.
        </p>

        <h3>Exalted Angel is the Universal Default</h3>
        <p>
            <a href="#" class="card-hover" data-card-name="Exalted Angel"
                >Exalted Angel</a
            > enters against virtually every non-combo opponent: aggro, control, and
            midrange alike. It stays out exclusively against opposing fast combo shells
            (Enchantress, mirrors, Elves), where diluting your internal combination
            velocity is fatal. When facing an unfamiliar non-combo list, bringing
            in Angel is universally strategically sound.
        </p>

        <h3>Frantic Search is the First Trim</h3>
        <p>
            <a href="#" class="card-hover" data-card-name="Frantic Search"
                >Frantic Search</a
            > is consistently trimmed against dedicated blue control and discard-heavy
            architectures where inherent card disadvantage (-1 card on resolution)
            proves overly taxing. Conversely, retain full sets against rapid aggro
            and combo where turn-three execution velocity is paramount.
        </p>

        <h3 style="margin-bottom: 0.5rem;">Core Trimming Rules Post-Board</h3>
        <ul style="padding-left: 1.25rem; margin-bottom: 0;">
            <li style="margin-bottom: 0.5rem;">
                <strong>Against Blue Control:</strong> Trim <a
                    href="#"
                    class="card-hover"
                    data-card-name="Intuition">Intuition</a
                > and <a
                    href="#"
                    class="card-hover"
                    data-card-name="Frantic Search">Frantic Search</a
                > to eliminate exploitative 2-for-1 counter trades.
            </li>
            <li style="margin-bottom: 0.5rem;">
                <strong>Against Non-Creature Shells:</strong> Reduce <a
                    href="#"
                    class="card-hover"
                    data-card-name="Swords to Plowshares"
                    >Swords to Plowshares</a
                > counts. However, exercise vigilance—many control pilots introduce
                creature clocks (<a
                    href="#"
                    class="card-hover"
                    data-card-name="Meddling Mage">Meddling Mage</a
                >, <a href="#" class="card-hover" data-card-name="Exalted Angel"
                    >Exalted Angel</a
                >) post-board, making it mathematically optimal to retain 1 or 2
                removal copies.
            </li>
            <li style="margin-bottom: 0;">
                <strong>Against Red Blast Suites (Sligh, Ponza):</strong> Trim expensive
                or card-disadvantaged blue engines like <a
                    href="#"
                    class="card-hover"
                    data-card-name="Intuition">Intuition</a
                > and <a
                    href="#"
                    class="card-hover"
                    data-card-name="Frantic Search">Frantic Search</a
                > to deny opponents devastating tempo trades against one-mana blasts like <a
                    href="#"
                    class="card-hover"
                    data-card-name="Pyroblast">Pyroblast</a
                > or Red Elemental Blast.
            </li>
        </ul>
    </Card>

    <Card title="Sideboard Card Analysis" titleTag="h2">
        <CardAnalysis title="Defense Grid" image="artifacts/defense_grid.jpg">
            <p style="margin-top: 0;">
                The premier resource equalizer against permission-heavy
                structures. By forcing opponents to pay an additional three mana
                to play spells during your turn, it neutralizes countermagic and
                allows your four-mana combo setups to deploy uninhibited.
                Furthermore, it operates seamlessly alongside cycling <a
                    href="#"
                    class="card-hover"
                    data-card-name="Decree of Silence">Decree of Silence</a
                > on opposing end steps.
            </p>
        </CardAnalysis>

        <CardAnalysis title="Exalted Angel" image="creatures/exalted_angel.jpg">
            <p style="margin-top: 0;">
                The quintessential post-sideboard hammer, introduced across
                virtually every non-combo matchup. Against red aggression, even
                deploying it as a face-down morph blocker that absorbs a burn
                spell preserves critical life. Against control, it demands
                physical removal that opponents frequently trimmed after Game 1.
                Against mana-denial builds (Terrageddon), establishing an early
                Angel clock consistently deters opponents from executing <a
                    href="#"
                    class="card-hover"
                    data-card-name="Armageddon">Armageddon</a
                >.
            </p>
            <p>
                <strong>The Transformational Advantage:</strong> Because opponents
                predictably side out spot removal in favor of enchantment sweepers
                and graveyard extraction (<a
                    href="#"
                    class="card-hover"
                    data-card-name="Aura of Silence">Aura of Silence</a
                >, <a
                    href="#"
                    class="card-hover"
                    data-card-name="Tormod's Crypt">Tormod's Crypt</a
                >), an early morph transition frequently dominates UW Control,
                Rock, and Ponza while they clutch inert hate pieces.
            </p>
        </CardAnalysis>

        <CardAnalysis title="Abeyance" image="instants/abeyance.jpg">
            <p style="margin-top: 0;">
                A multi-functional protection cantrip that secures your combo
                turn by silencing opposing instant spells and activated non-mana
                abilities. Fielded aggressively against deep permission decks
                and during combo races where guaranteeing an uninterruptible
                operational phase decides the championship.
            </p>
        </CardAnalysis>

        <CardAnalysis title="Arcane Denial" image="instants/arcane_denial.jpg">
            <p style="margin-top: 0;">
                An exceptional tactical tool when evaluated through advanced
                resource math. In isolation, <a
                    href="#"
                    class="card-hover"
                    data-card-name="Arcane Denial">Arcane Denial</a
                > grants the opponent net card advantage (+1 total). However, championship
                games hinge upon tempo and game-state preservation: intercepting an
                opposing <a
                    href="#"
                    class="card-hover"
                    data-card-name="Armageddon">Armageddon</a
                > or lethal win condition is vastly superior to conceding massive
                board destruction. Similarly, protecting your own decisive <a
                    href="#"
                    class="card-hover"
                    data-card-name="Replenish">Replenish</a
                > justifies granting the opponent delayed cards when resolution immediately
                locks the table.
            </p>
        </CardAnalysis>

        <CardAnalysis title="Hydroblast" image="instants/hydroblast.jpg">
            <p style="margin-top: 0;">
                Precision anti-red interaction engineered specifically for
                Goblins and Sligh architectures. Intercepting a turn-one <a
                    href="#"
                    class="card-hover"
                    data-card-name="Goblin Lackey">Goblin Lackey</a
                > or neutralising high-damage burn trajectories provides essential
                early game survival.
            </p>
        </CardAnalysis>

        <h3 style="margin-top: 2.5rem; margin-bottom: 0.5rem;">
            Flex Slots (1 Mage / 1 Seal / 1 Swords / 1 Web)
        </h3>
        <p style="margin-bottom: 0;">
            These designated sideboard utility slots accommodate additional
            copies of foundational maindeck tools: deploying a fourth <a
                href="#"
                class="card-hover"
                data-card-name="Meddling Mage">Meddling Mage</a
            > against blue control, a fourth <a
                href="#"
                class="card-hover"
                data-card-name="Seal of Cleansing">Seal of Cleansing</a
            > against Enchantress mirrors, a fourth <a
                href="#"
                class="card-hover"
                data-card-name="Swords to Plowshares">Swords to Plowshares</a
            > against aggressive beatdown, or supplementary <a
                href="#"
                class="card-hover"
                data-card-name="Tsabo's Web">Tsabo's Web</a
            > against land-denial suites.
        </p>
    </Card>

    <Card title="Other Sideboard Considerations" titleTag="h2">
        <CardAnalysis title="Orim's Chant" image="instants/orims_chant.webp">
            <p style="margin-top: 0;">
                In the post-<a
                    href="#"
                    class="card-hover"
                    data-card-name="Parallax Tide">Parallax Tide</a
                > era, utilizing <a
                    href="#"
                    class="card-hover"
                    data-card-name="Orim's Chant">Orim's Chant</a
                > purely to force through individual engine pieces incurs significant
                card disadvantage without guaranteeing an immediate hard lockout.
                Tournament practice demonstrates that <a
                    href="#"
                    class="card-hover"
                    data-card-name="Defense Grid">Defense Grid</a
                > provides far superior, permanent structural resilience against Landstill,
                Psychatog, and Stiflenought formations.
            </p>
        </CardAnalysis>

        <CardAnalysis title="Wrath of God" image="sorceries/wrath_of_god.webp">
            <p style="margin-top: 0;">
                An elite defensive sweeper against swarming creature tribal
                structures like Goblins and Elves. Furthermore, it functions as
                clean answer to opposing <a
                    href="#"
                    class="card-hover"
                    data-card-name="Meddling Mage">Meddling Mage</a
                > configurations targeting spot removal or combo pieces. Highly recommended
                whenever fast tribal formations spike in regional meta representation.
            </p>
        </CardAnalysis>

        <CardAnalysis
            title="Sphere of Law"
            image="enchantments/sphere_of_law.jpg"
        >
            <p style="margin-top: 0;">
                The absolute terminal answer against mono-red Sligh and burn
                strategies. Mitigating two points of damage across every
                individual red source establishes an almost unbreachable
                defensive buffer. While modern spot removal counts sufficiently
                handle average burn presence, dedicating sideboard space to
                Sphere is paramount if Sligh saturates your meta.
            </p>
        </CardAnalysis>

        <CardAnalysis title="Alter Reality" image="instants/alter_reality.jpg">
            <p style="margin-top: 0;">
                Highly precise regional tech deployed specifically to counter <a
                    href="#"
                    class="card-hover"
                    data-card-name="Gloom">Gloom</a
                > from black disruptive piles. By modulating the word "White" to "Black"
                on their resolved Gloom, the tax reverses entirely onto the opponent's
                own spell curve. Its embedded Flashback cost ensures functional utility
                even under discarded constraints.
            </p>
        </CardAnalysis>

        <CardAnalysis title="Disenchant" image="instants/disenchant.jpg">
            <p style="margin-top: 0;">
                <strong>The Seal/Disenchant Split:</strong> With the dominance of
                <a href="#" class="card-hover" data-card-name="Aura of Silence"
                    >Aura of Silence</a
                > across contemporary tournaments, relying exclusively on <a
                    href="#"
                    class="card-hover"
                    data-card-name="Seal of Cleansing">Seal of Cleansing</a
                > post-board presents vulnerability to two-mana static taxation. Maintaining
                1 or 2 copies of <a
                    href="#"
                    class="card-hover"
                    data-card-name="Disenchant">Disenchant</a
                > provides immune, instant-speed interaction that cleanly bypasses
                Aura's cost penalty.
            </p>
        </CardAnalysis>

        <CardAnalysis
            title="Nevinyrral's Disk"
            image="artifacts/nevinyrrals_disk.jpg"
        >
            <p style="margin-top: 0;">
                The premier universal reset button against intricate permanent
                locks, heavy midrange boards, and resolved anti-combo artifacts.
                In protracted attrition battles (e.g., The Rock, UW Control),
                detonating an active Disk not only wipes opposing creatures and
                hate pieces simultaneously, but milimetrically drops your own
                fielded enchantments directly into your graveyard—preparing a
                devastating follow-up <a
                    href="#"
                    class="card-hover"
                    data-card-name="Replenish">Replenish</a
                > onto a completely clear board.
            </p>
        </CardAnalysis>

        <CardAnalysis title="Chill" image="enchantments/chill.jpg">
            <p style="margin-top: 0;">
                An absolute tournament powerhouse against aggressive red decks,
                Sligh, and Burn. By doubling the casting cost of every red spell,
                it completely paralyzes early burn velocity and renders high-cost
                threats untranslatable. Because it exists as an enchantment, any
                discarded copies naturally recur during late-game combo loops.
            </p>
        </CardAnalysis>

        <CardAnalysis
            title="Light of Day"
            image="enchantments/light_of_day.jpg"
        >
            <p style="margin-top: 0;">
                The ultimate structural lockdown against black creature-driven
                archetypes. Against Suicide Black, Psychatog, or aggressive The
                Rock formations, deploying a resolved <a
                    href="#"
                    class="card-hover"
                    data-card-name="Light of Day">Light of Day</a
                > establishes an absolute prohibition: black creatures are permanently
                forbidden from attacking or blocking. Furthermore, its four-mana
                enchantment card type ensures seamless recursion with <a
                    href="#"
                    class="card-hover"
                    data-card-name="Replenish">Replenish</a
                > if stripped early by opposing discard suites.
            </p>
        </CardAnalysis>
    </Card>

    <Card title="Navigating Opposing Sideboard Hate" titleTag="h2">
        <p style="margin-top: 0; margin-bottom: 0;">
            Once you have constructed your sideboard architecture and mastered
            your strategic transformations, the ultimate test of competitive
            proficiency is anticipating and out-sequencing your opponent's
            targeted hate in Games 2 and 3. For a comprehensive, card-by-card
            masterclass on defeating graveyard extraction, taxing structures,
            and enchantment sweepers, proceed to our dedicated <a
                href="/playing-through-hate"
                style="color: var(--w-color-info); text-decoration: underline; font-weight: bold;"
                >Playing Through Hate analysis</a
            >.
        </p>
    </Card>
    <DecklistTooltip />
</Layout>
